There is a viable native route after all. I can turn the paper’s “all but finitely many” argument into an executable certificate: for each of the 15 nonzero subset sums of the four points, a small good-reduction prime certifies that its reduction is not a double; a separate reduction certifies no rational 2-torsion. Then I can generate infinitely many auxiliary-curve points in fixed congruence classes, carrying those certificates to every instance. I’m testing that construction now—especially the subtle good-reduction and denominator conditions—before committing to it.
